For $matbold(A) = mat(0, -1; 1, 0)$, representing a $90 degree$ rotation of $CC^2$, the characteristic polynomial is $chi_matbold(A)(t) = t^2 + 1$, so the eigenvalues are $lambda_1 = ii$ and $lambda_2 = -ii$, with eigenvectors $alpha vec(1, -ii)$ and $beta vec(1, ii)$ respectively.

For $matbold(A) = mat(1, 1; 0, 1)$ over $RR^2$, $chi_matbold(A)(t) = (t - 1)^2$: the single eigenvalue $lambda = 1$ has multiplicity $2$, yet the eigenvectors are only $bold(v) = alpha vec(1, 0)$, a single line.
